 ## Background
-The technique originated as a reference method for evaluating direct isosurface rendering pipelines [S3, unofficial]. It was introduced in works such as "Efficient Direct Isosurface Rasterization of Scalar Volumes" [S1, S2][S3, unofficial] and "Interactive Isosurface Ray Tracing of Large Octree Volumes" [S4, unofficial]. It was designed to provide a fair comparison against novel rasterization-based approaches by using a well-understood, GPU-friendly acceleration structure (min-max octree) that avoids the preprocessing requirements of Sphere Tracing or the limitations of Segment Tracing [S2][S3, unofficial]. The method leverages the duality between voxels and cells, mapping each voxel to the lower-left corner of a cell to facilitate ray tracing [S4, unofficial].
+The method originates from the work of Knoll et al., who combined the min/max acceleration functions of Wilhelms and Van Gelder [WV92] with a compressed format similar to the "cells octree" of Velasco and Torres [VT01] [S5, unofficial]. Knoll et al. built the octree directly around voxels, requiring a fast neighbor-finding scheme (borrowed and improved from Frisken and Perry [FP02]) to retrieve cell corner values during ray tracing [S5, unofficial]. Ray traversal is performed on the min/max octree structure using a traversal similar to that proposed by Gargantini and Atkinson [GA93] [S5, unofficial]. The method is used as a baseline reference for direct isosurface visualization performance in evaluations [S3, unofficial].
 
